    "turn_text": "i i think you're right and almost all the problems ironically can be solved by simply going back to the basic set of principles. And I mean spiritually and economically. The economics part of it is the secondary part of it. I think we've all got to realize that I've said before when we've talked that The very core of our, in by our way, our founding fathers even said that when they said that this constitution in way of free government was designed only for a moral and religious people. It cannot be sustained by any other. That's just a rough interpretation. But here's the whole thing. Faith exists in certain very strongly in certain parts of the world, in certain parts of time. And it's not a coincidence that if you begin to look at these different, you think they're totally disparate, but they are not. They have similar cultural and conditions that allow for the thriving of faith. And even if you don't believe in religion or faith, you are the beneficiary of the results of it. And that is a life guided by principles versus feeling. And I don't mean that we want a suppressed feeling because I think of principles being an artist, a fine artist. I think of principles like a black and white, like a line drawing on it. A life without principles is total chaos. And the feelings are the color and that you have in the painting. It enriches the line drawing and adds passion and joy and awe to it all. But color without principles or line drawing is chaos as well. And so it is the blending of these two that add richness and structure to a life. And that principles do not suppress feelings. It allows for the free expression of it because you now not self-destructing. In a quick example, I can give hundreds. But let's say you have a child that is having struggling in school. Is it compassionate to let them avoid their fears? Or is it more compassionate to seem like you're being more strict and adding more structure and discipline into that so that two years from now they can see the joy of success and how they have advanced, and then they become more mighty and powerful. A compassionate person, unguided by principle, would let them fall into their weakness and succumb to their fears, and they would have to wallow in it the rest of their lives. And that's not true compassion. And so our societies, the societies that are guided by these principles and ours was, and we were the most religious place on earth, We were then the free enterprise and capitalism was the natural expression of that culture. It does not come first. It does not supplant their spiritual and principal basis of the society is the backbone that allows free enterprise to most thrive. And in those conditions, we were strong. And we were compassionate. And the thing that, oh, God, now my brain just went blank. That Frenchman in the 1840s that came over and studied us. Tocqueville. One of the things, what's that? Tocqueville. Tocqueville, thank you very much. I knew that on the tip of my tongue. The thing that so impressed him was that we, the local community, we turned to our bishops, we turned to our minister, we turned to the local community. We have the internal strength to handle our problems. We look nowhere else than ourselves to take care of the ordinary. And we did, my grandparents and my dad did not think of mental disease and problems they were concerned about. Am I guided by the principles and living in those principles? And life was short oftentimes. Dad said when he was a boy, their main concern is having food through the winter. It was not nasal gay, navel gazing into these tiny little problems we have today. He had to watch his sisters die three of them. He held them as they died from the influenza. They were close to death and they were close to the richness and joy of life through spiritual principles. And I think too often as we become more shallow and we fall away to personal pleasure, and we we try to find pleasure and spirituality try to find pleasure here and pleasure there we actually get left pleasure and we end up coming to more emotional neurotic system but that's generally what i'm talking about",
